# Cursor Rules Guide

How to create and manage Cursor rules in this project. Based on [official Cursor documentation](https://cursor.com/docs/context/rules).

## Rule Location

Always place rule files in `PROJECT_ROOT/.cursor/rules/`:

```
.cursor/rules/
├── your-rule-name.mdc
├── another-rule.mdc
└── ...
```

## Naming Convention

- Use **kebab-case** for filenames
- Always use **.mdc** extension
- Make names descriptive of the rule's purpose
- Examples: `server-api.mdc`, `ui-components.mdc`, `testing.mdc`

## Rule Structure

Each rule file uses **MDC** format with frontmatter metadata:

```md
---
description: Short description of the rule's purpose
globs: optional/path/pattern/**/*
alwaysApply: false
---

# Rule Title

Main content explaining the rule with markdown formatting.

## Section 1

Content here with examples.

## Section 2

More content...
```

### Frontmatter Properties

- **`description`**: Brief description used for intelligent rule matching
- **`globs`**: File path pattern (e.g., `server/**/*.ts`, `app/components/**/*.vue`)
- **`alwaysApply`**: Set to `true` to apply to every chat session

## Rule Types

Configure rule type in Cursor Settings → Rules, which controls how rules are applied:

| Rule Type                   | When Applied                     | Use Case                                |
| --------------------------- | -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| **Always Apply**            | Every chat session               | Critical project-wide standards         |
| **Apply Intelligently**     | When Agent decides it's relevant | Domain-specific knowledge               |
| **Apply to Specific Files** | When file matches glob pattern   | File-type specific guidelines           |
| **Apply Manually**          | When @-mentioned                 | Templates, workflows, rarely-used rules |

## Best Practices

Follow these guidelines from [Cursor's official documentation](https://cursor.com/docs/context/rules):

### 1. Keep Rules Focused and Actionable

Rules should be focused, actionable, and scoped to specific domains.

```md
## <!-- ✅ Good: Focused rule for server code -->

globs: server/\*_/_.ts
description: Server and API implementation guidelines

---

# Server API Guidelines

Specific guidelines for server-side code...

## <!-- ❌ Bad: Too broad, tries to cover everything -->

## description: All coding guidelines

# Everything About Coding

Frontend, backend, testing, deployment, everything...
```

### 2. Keep Rules Under 500 Lines

If a rule exceeds 500 lines, split it into multiple composable rules.

```md
<!-- ✅ Good: Split into focused rules -->

.cursor/rules/
├── server-api.mdc # Server/API guidelines
├── server-security.mdc # Security-specific rules
└── server-testing.mdc # Testing patterns

<!-- ❌ Bad: One massive rule file -->

.cursor/rules/
└── server-everything.mdc # 2000+ lines covering everything
```

### 3. Provide Concrete Examples

Include concrete code examples showing good vs bad patterns.

````md
<!-- ✅ Good: Concrete examples -->

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Validate all inputs
const walletAddress = getRouterParam(event, "walletAddress");
if (!walletAddress || !isAddress(walletAddress)) {
  throw createError({ statusCode: 400, statusMessage: "Invalid address" });
}

// ❌ Bad: No validation
const walletAddress = getRouterParam(event, "walletAddress");
```
````

````

### 4. Reference Files When Helpful

Use `@filename.ts` to include actual project files in rule context.

```md
<!-- ✅ Good: Reference actual project files -->
Reference existing code for patterns:

- `@server/api/portfolio/[walletAddress].get.ts` - API handler example
- `@server/services/portfolio.service.ts` - Service layer example
````

### 5. Write Like Clear Internal Docs

Avoid vague guidance. Write rules as clear, actionable documentation.

```md
<!-- ✅ Good: Clear, specific guidance -->

Always validate route parameters before processing:

1. Extract parameter using `getRouterParam(event, "paramName")`
2. Validate format using appropriate library (e.g., `isAddress` from viem)
3. Throw `createError` with status 400 if invalid
4. Process only after validation passes

<!-- ❌ Bad: Vague guidance -->

Make sure inputs are valid before using them.
```

### 6. Reuse Rules When Repeating Prompts

If you find yourself repeating the same prompts in chat, create a rule instead.

```md
## <!-- ✅ Good: Create reusable rule -->

description: Template for creating new API endpoints
globs: server/api/\*_/_.ts

---

# API Endpoint Template

When creating new API endpoints:

1. Validate inputs first
2. Call service layer
3. Handle errors appropriately
4. Return DTOs

<!-- ❌ Bad: Keep repeating same prompt in chat -->

User: "Create an API endpoint following our patterns..."
[AI creates endpoint]
User: "Create another endpoint following our patterns..."
[AI creates endpoint again]
```

## Nested Rules

Organize rules by placing them in `.cursor/rules` directories throughout your project:

```
project/
  .cursor/rules/           # Project-wide rules
  backend/
    server/
      .cursor/rules/       # Backend-specific rules
  frontend/
    .cursor/rules/         # Frontend-specific rules
```

Nested rules automatically attach when files in their directory are referenced.

## Creating Rules

### Method 1: Cursor Settings

1. Go to **Cursor Settings → Rules**
2. Click **New Cursor Rule**
3. Configure rule type, description, and globs
4. Write rule content

### Method 2: Manual Creation

1. Create file in `.cursor/rules/your-rule-name.mdc`
2. Add frontmatter with metadata
3. Write rule content following best practices

### Method 3: Ask Agent

Ask the agent in chat: "Create a new rule for [purpose]"

## Examples

Reference existing rules in this project:

